<v Ira         Glass>[音乐播放]

00:19:03.320 --> 00:19:51.230
<v Ira Glass>Act Two, "The Real Decoy." So now we turn to the story of a man who had always had one way of doing things and talking to people. And then he did not need a stranger on the street to walk up to him with a microphone. He noticed this on his own, that the way he was dealing with people had stopped working the way it always had, and he made some adjustments. Stephanie Foo tells what happened.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，“真正的诱饵”。现在我们要讲述一个男人，他一直有一种做事和与人交谈的方式。然后，他不需要一个街上的陌生人拿着麦克风向他走来。他自己注意到了，他与人交往的方式已经不再奏效了，于是他做了一些调整。斯蒂芬妮·傅讲述了发生的事情。

00:19:51.230 --> 00:20:02.980
<v Stephanie Foo>Michael Pitre was always the new kid growing up . He went to 10 schools in five states. But he generally did OK, because he's always been good at telling stories. Even now, he's got a solid arsenal.
<v Stephanie Foo>迈克尔·皮特雷在成长过程中一直是“新来的孩子”。他在五个州读过10所学校。但他总体上做得不错，因为他一直很擅长讲故事。即使现在，他也有丰富的库存。

00:20:02.980 --> 00:20:06.890
<v Michael Pitre>Do you want to hear Fred the Scorpion? That's another one of the greatest hits.
<v Michael Pitre>你想听“蝎子弗雷德”吗？那是另一个最热门的故事。

00:20:06.890 --> 00:20:07.490
<v Stephanie Foo>I--
<v Stephanie Foo>我——

00:20:07.490 --> 00:20:09.750
<v Michael Pitre>Do you want to hear something else? Want to hear another funny one?
<v Michael Pitre>你想听别的吗？想听另一个有趣的？

00:20:09.750 --> 00:20:11.890
<v Stephanie Foo>I actually-- I'm running out of time a little bit.
<v Stephanie Foo>其实——我的时间有点不够了。

00:20:11.890 --> 00:20:28.900
<v Ira Glass>Mike is like this wherever he is-- at home, at work. In 2006 and 2007, Mike served two tours in Iraq, and he had a rep there as the storyteller, the funny guy. Joslyn Hemler was in the Marine Corps with them, and I asked her about turnover briefs.
<v Ira Glass>迈克无论在哪里都是这样——在家、在工作。2006年和2007年，迈克两次被派往伊拉克，他在那里以故事大王、幽默达人的身份闻名。乔斯林·海姆勒和他一起在海军陆战队服役，我问了她关于交接简报的事。

00:20:28.900 --> 00:20:35.260
<v Ira Glass>Mike ran all the operations for his battalion overnight, and in the morning had to give a briefing of the events of the past 12 hours.
<v Ira Glass>迈克整晚负责营里的所有行动，早上还要进行过去12小时的简报。

00:20:35.260 --> 00:21:05.590
<v Joslyn Hemler>Turnover briefs are one of those monotonous things we probably do. So very matter of fact, very military. But Mike, it somehow turned into like, a quasi like, soap opera of events. He'd be like, sitting in this rolling chair, he'd have one of those extended pointers. And he'd be like, let's go to the map. And he'd like, roll all the way across the watch floor. Like, he just gave such rich detail to the story.
<v Joslyn Hemler>交接简报通常是我们做的最枯燥的事情之一。所以非常公事公办，非常军事化。但迈克不知怎么把它变成了某种肥皂剧。他会坐在那张转椅上，拿着那种伸缩指示棒。他会说：“让我们看地图。”然后他会直接滚过整个观察室。他给故事增添了极其丰富的细节。

00:21:05.590 --> 00:21:15.950
<v Joslyn Hemler>A lot of times, he's looking for validation when he tells his stories, which are funny. He'll be like, that was funny, right?
<v Joslyn Hemler>很多时候，他在讲那些有趣的故事时是在寻求认可。他会说：那很有趣吧，对吧？

00:21:15.950 --> 00:21:26.320
<v Stephanie  Foo>Mike came back stateside after his first deployment, and he was visiting his parents on New Year's Eve, as some kids were shooting fireworks.
<v Stephanie  Foo>迈克在第一次部署后回到国内，新年夜去探望父母时，一些孩子正在放烟花。

00:21:26.320 --> 00:21:34.160
<v Michael Pitre>I said, yeah, those fireworks look like airburst mortars. And my brother said, oh, did you see any airburst mortars? I mean, a question as innocuous as that.
<v Michael Pitre>我说：“是啊，那些烟花看起来像空爆迫击炮。”我哥哥说：“哦，你见过空爆迫击炮吗？”我的天，这样一个极其普通的问题。

00:21:34.160 --> 00:21:39.330
<v Stephanie Foo>So Mike did what he'd always done. He started telling the first story that popped into his head on the subject.
<v Stephanie Foo>于是迈克做了他一贯的做法。他开始讲脑海中蹦出来的第一个关于这个话题的故事。

00:21:39.330 --> 00:21:57.290
<v Michael Pitre>And I started talking about-- there was this one day I was running on this large base where I was stationed, and a volley of airburst mortars came in. And where they went off, right underneath them was a Naval officer, and she took a piece of shrapnel right through her neck, right in front of me and these three other Marines.
<v Michael Pitre>我开始讲述——有一天我在我驻扎的大基地里跑步，一轮空爆迫击炮打了进来。就在它们爆炸的地方，下面正好是一名海军军官，她就在我和其他三名陆战队员面前，被一块弹片穿透了脖子。

00:21:57.290 --> 00:22:12.470
<v Michael Pitre>And she is dying, she is bleeding to death, and she is scared. And it's me and these three Marines getting her into the back of a Humvee, and she's telling us what to tell her kids. And telling that to my family--
<v Michael Pitre>她正在死去，正在流血致死，而且她很害怕。是我和另外三名陆战队员把她抬进悍马车后座，她告诉我们要转告她孩子们什么话。把这些讲给我的家人听——

00:22:12.470 --> 00:22:16.680
<v Stephanie   Foo>What did their faces look like as you were saying it?
<v Stephanie   Foo>你讲这些时，他们脸上的表情是什么样的？

00:22:16.680 --> 00:22:25.810
<v Michael Pitre>Just sort of shocked and horrified. And you don't ever want your family to look at you with shock and horror. That's not fun.
<v Michael Pitre>就是那种震惊和恐惧。你永远不希望你的家人用震惊和恐惧的眼神看你。那一点也不好玩。

00:22:25.810 --> 00:22:40.350
<v Stephanie Foo>Mike told his family, by the way, she wound up being OK. The officer survived. But that didn't lighten the mood. And he couldn't figure out how to save the moment. That was new and weird.
<v Stephanie Foo>顺便说一句，迈克告诉家人，她最终没事。那位军官活了下来。但那并没有缓解气氛。他无法弄清楚如何拯救当时的局面。那是新鲜且诡异的。

00:22:40.350 --> 00:22:57.690
<v Stephanie Foo>This happened a few more times when he was home between deployments. His best friend Brock asked him about IEDs. And he answered honestly. They never talked about IEDs again after that. Other friends on other nights at other bars asked about Iraqis or politics.
<v Stephanie Foo>他在部署间隙回家时，这种情况又发生了几次。他最好的朋友布洛克问他关于IED（简易爆炸装置）的事。他诚实地回答了。那之后他们再也没谈过IED的事。其他朋友在其他夜晚、其他酒吧问起伊拉克人或政治。

00:22:57.690 --> 00:23:18.190
<v Michael Pitre>And so the first time your friends from back home ask you, you answer the question very, very honestly. And you realize that halfway through your answer, that you're making everyone very uncomfortable. And it's almost like the air between you starts to thicken. It's almost a physical feeling of people withdrawing from you.
<v Michael Pitre>所以，当老家的朋友第一次问你时，你非常非常诚实地回答了问题。然后你意识到在回答到一半时，你让每个人都非常不舒服。空气仿佛都凝固了。那是一种人们退缩的物理感觉。

00:23:18.190 --> 00:23:25.900
<v Michael Pitre>What are they supposed to do? What are they supposed to say to you? They have nothing to say to you. And also, what are they thinking about me right now?
<v Michael Pitre>他们应该做什么？他们应该对你说什么？他们对你无话可说。而且，他们现在在想我什么？

00:23:25.900 --> 00:23:29.720
<v Stephanie  Foo>What were you afraid that they were thinking about you?
<v Stephanie  Foo>你害怕他们想你什么？

00:23:29.720 --> 00:23:51.440
<v Michael Pitre>That I was damaged. That I had nothing else to talk about. That I was a violent person. That I wasn't a normal person. And if you say I don't want to talk about it, then you're the truculent vet who is going to be sitting brooding in the corner, and that carries its own stigma.
<v Michael Pitre>觉得我受损了。觉得我没别的话可聊。觉得我是一个暴力的人。觉得我不是个正常人。如果你说不想聊这个，那你就是那个倔强的退伍军人，坐在角落里沉思，这本身就带有污名。

00:23:51.440 --> 00:23:55.110
<v Stephanie Foo>We think that it's like, oh, it's because you're so damaged.
<v Stephanie Foo>我们认为，噢，这是因为你太受损了。

00:23:55.110 --> 00:23:58.110
<v Michael Pitre>Right. You have a shattered psyche. You must.
<v Michael Pitre>没错。你的心理破碎了。肯定是的。

00:23:58.110 --> 00:24:01.440
<v Stephanie Foo>But you're like, no, it's because I'm trying to protect your psyche.
<v Stephanie Foo>但你会说：不，这是因为我在试图保护你们的心理。

00:24:01.440 --> 00:24:13.680
<v Michael Pitre>I want to protect the conversation. I think what people hear sometimes is "I don't want to talk about it because it bothers me so much." And sometimes it's, "I don't want to talk about it because it's going to bother you, and you don't even know it."
<v Michael Pitre>我想保护对话。我认为人们有时听到的是：“我不想谈，因为这让我太烦恼了。”而有时其实是：“我不想谈，因为这会让你烦恼，而你甚至还没意识到。”

00:24:13.680 --> 00:24:33.760
<v Stephanie  Foo>He learned to shut up about that stuff. Not that people stopped asking. Like he was out with friends, and someone asked him about mortars again. And even though, of course, the first story that popped into his head was about the officer who got shrapnel in her neck, it was like he went on to the next slide. Tried something different. And he blurted out--
<v Stephanie  Foo>他学会了闭嘴不谈这些事。并不是人们不再问了。就像他和朋友出去时，有人又问他关于迫击炮的事。尽管他脑子里蹦出的第一个故事显然是关于脖子被弹片击中的军官，但他就像跳到了下一张幻灯片。尝试了一些不同的做法。他脱口而出——

00:24:33.760 --> 00:24:41.760
<v Michael Pitre>Well, you know, you have to get a good poo on the mortar. They're like, oh, what, a poo on the mortar? It's like, yeah, the point of origin.
<v Michael Pitre>嗯，你知道，你得查清迫击炮的POO（发射点）。他们说：哦，什么，迫击炮的排泄物（poo）？他说：是的，发射点。

00:24:41.760 --> 00:24:47.130
<v Stephanie Foo>Basically, they can trace where an enemy mortar's launched from. Its point of origin, POO.
<v Stephanie Foo>基本上，他们可以追踪敌方迫击炮是从哪里发射的。它的发射点，即POO。

00:24:47.130 --> 00:25:01.980
<v Michael Pitre>So everyone just refers very casually to a mortar's POO. And then in the command center, there's like wall maps, where there's an officer in charge of marking every POO in-- no kidding-- a little brown marker.
<v Michael Pitre>所以每个人都非常随意地提到迫击炮的POO。然后在指挥中心，有壁挂式地图，负责标记每一个POO的军官会用——不是开玩笑——一支棕色小记号笔标记。

00:25:01.980 --> 00:25:23.720
<v Michael Pitre>And there was this place to the northeast of Fallujah. It's a bend in the river. Topographically, it looked like a scrotum. So everyone called it the ball sack. And that's the place where most of the mortars were launched. And so, you'd see your old friends on the base. And you'd say, what are you up to these days? And they go, oh, you know, I'm just POO hunting in the ball sac. Yeah, be careful. Ball sac's no joke.
<v Michael Pitre>在费卢杰东北部有个地方。那是河的一个弯道。地形看起来像个阴囊。所以大家都叫它“蛋蛋袋”。那里是大多数迫击炮发射的地方。所以，当你看到基地里的老朋友时。你会问：最近忙什么呢？他们会说：哦，你知道，我只是在蛋蛋袋里搜寻POO。是的，小心点。蛋蛋袋可不是开玩笑的。

00:25:23.720 --> 00:25:44.330
<v Stephanie         Foo>He was back, the funny guy. The guy people actually wanted to be around. And it was easy. Mike started getting better at using questions to pivot to funny military stories. Like when people asked about helicopters, he talked about how a friend's epic farts would stink up the whole bird.
<v Stephanie         Foo>他又变回了那个有趣的家伙。大家真正愿意接近的那种人。而且这很容易。迈克开始擅长利用问题来引出有趣的军事故事。比如当人们问起直升机时，他就聊起朋友的史诗级屁是怎么让整个舱室发臭的。

00:25:44.330 --> 00:25:47.200
<v Stephanie Foo>Did it feel like you were connecting with people?
<v Stephanie Foo>你觉得你是在和人建立联系吗？

00:25:47.200 --> 00:26:00.460
<v Michael Pitre>Yes. It is euphoric. It was great for years. It felt great. There was an element of relief in having solved the problem.
<v Michael Pitre>是的。那种感觉是欣快。这很好，持续了好多年。感觉很棒。解决这个问题有一种解脱感。

00:26:00.460 --> 00:26:01.140
<v Stephanie Foo>Yeah.
<v Stephanie Foo>是的。

00:26:01.140 --> 00:26:10.910
<v Michael Pitre>That's what an officer does. You're handed a problem. In the military, it's called a mission. You design the solution to it, and you execute it.
<v Michael Pitre>这就是军官所做的。你被交办一个问题。在军事上，这被称为任务。你设计解决方案，然后执行。

00:26:10.910 --> 00:26:23.540
<v Stephanie Foo>It was around this time that Mike met Erin. She was a friend of his little sister's, and they'd started emailing each other during his first deployment. And he developed a huge crush, even though he'd never met her in person.
<v Stephanie Foo>就在那时，迈克遇到了艾琳。她是迈克小妹妹的朋友，他们在迈克的第一次部署期间开始互发邮件。他暗恋上了她，尽管从未见过面。

00:26:23.540 --> 00:26:56.700
<v Stephanie Foo>So now that he was home, he finally went out with her and a bunch of her friends. So they're out, and someone brought up the Iraqis. Said, are they even doing anything for themselves over there? This was such a cartoonish idea of the war, it annoyed Mike. And without thinking, he found himself telling a story to prove how seriously the Iraqi army took their jobs, a story where in order to find a terrorist, the Iraqi army rounded up all the men in a village, bound them, and put them on their knees with their heads against the wall. That's how seriously they took this.
<v Stephanie Foo>所以现在他回家了，他终于和她以及她的一群朋友出去玩。他们在外面，有人提到了伊拉克人。说：他们在那里真的在为自己做点什么吗？这对于战争是一个非常卡通的想法，让迈克很烦。他没多想，发现自己在讲一个故事，为了证明伊拉克军队有多认真对待工作，故事内容是：为了找到恐怖分子，伊拉克军队把村里所有的男人抓起来，捆住，让他们跪在墙边。这就是他们对待这项工作的认真程度。

00:26:56.700 --> 00:27:13.160
<v Stephanie Foo>He stopped talking and realized, crickets. He looked up at Erin. She was horrified. And he thought, oh god, oh god. I've got to tell a funny story now. So he launched into one of his classics, Fred the Scorpion, which goes like this.
<v Stephanie Foo>他停下谈话，意识到：鸦雀无声。他抬头看艾琳。她被吓坏了。他想：天哪，天哪。我得讲个有趣的故事了。于是他开始了经典故事之一，“蝎子弗雷德”，内容是这样的。

00:27:13.160 --> 00:27:27.580
<v Michael Pitre>So my first deployment, I was a platoon commander. And some of my Marines found a scorpion. And that's a big deal. You can feed it vermin. You can fight it against other scorpions owned by other platoons.
<v Michael Pitre>我第一次部署时，是排长。我的一些陆战队员发现了一只蝎子。这可是大事。你可以用害虫喂它。你可以让它和其他排拥有的蝎子打架。

00:27:27.580 --> 00:27:36.980
<v Michael Pitre>So this scorpion, unbeknownst to me, kind of became the platoon mascot, and they kept it in a cardboard box in their barracks, right underneath someone's bed.
<v Michael Pitre>所以这只蝎子，我不知道，成了排里的吉祥物，他们把它关在营房的一个纸箱里，就在某人的床底下。

00:27:36.980 --> 00:27:50.140
<v Stephanie Foo>Mike finds out about the scorpion. Turns out, it's one of the most deadly species in the world. He's their boss, so he orders them to kill it. But everybody is really attached to Fred, so they come up with this crazy plan to euthanize him.
<v Stephanie Foo>迈克发现了蝎子的事。结果发现，这是世界上最致命的品种之一。他是他们的上司，所以他命令他们杀死它。但每个人都对弗雷德产生了深厚感情，所以他们想出了这个疯狂的计划来安乐死它。

00:27:50.140 --> 00:28:02.860
<v Michael Pitre>They dropped him into a bucket of diesel fuel for, I think, three days. And he's all shriveled up, and everyone's pretty convinced he's dead. They pick him up, he springs back to life, and he runs off into the desert.
<v Michael Pitre>我想，他们把它扔进了一桶柴油里，大概三天。它缩成一团，所有人都确信它死了。他们把它捡起来，它竟然死而复生，逃进了沙漠。

00:28:02.860 --> 00:28:03.910
<v Michael Pitre>[LAUGHING]
<v Michael Pitre>[笑声]

00:28:03.910 --> 00:28:06.380
<v Michael Pitre>Yeah, Fred the Scorpion. I miss him.
<v Michael Pitre>是啊，蝎子弗雷德。我很想念它。

00:28:06.380 --> 00:28:09.300
<v Stephanie Foo>OK. So Fred the Scorpion.
<v Stephanie Foo>好吧。这就是蝎子弗雷德。

00:28:09.300 --> 00:28:11.300
<v Michael Pitre>Yeah. It goes over great.
<v Michael Pitre>是的。效果很好。

00:28:11.300 --> 00:28:14.390
<v Stephanie Foo>Is it a decoy for another story that is real?
<v Stephanie Foo>这是另一个真实故事的诱饵吗？

00:28:14.390 --> 00:28:28.090
<v Michael  Pitre>Yeah. And the story-- so there was a curfew at night in the surrounding communities, like [INAUDIBLE], and Fallujah, and Ramadi. So people couldn't drive at night.
<v Michael  Pitre>是的。那个故事——当时周边社区有宵禁，比如[听不清]、费卢杰和拉马迪。所以人们晚上不能开车。

00:28:28.090 --> 00:28:38.120
<v Stephanie Foo>Power was intermittent, and it was hot. Some Iraqi families would sleep on the roof at night to keep cool. It wasn't unheard of for kids to get stung by scorpions up there.
<v Stephanie Foo>电力是断断续续的，而且天气很热。一些伊拉克家庭晚上会在屋顶睡觉以保持凉爽。孩子们在那里被蝎子蜇伤并不是什么闻所未闻的事。

00:28:38.120 --> 00:28:58.580
<v Michael Pitre>And they would be faced with the decision of, do we get in our car and try to take our child to get medical help and risk getting shot? And that's something I heard all the time from Iraqis. What do we do if our child is dying of a scorpion bite? Can we violate curfew then?
<v Michael Pitre>他们将面临抉择：我们要开车带孩子去看病，冒着被枪杀的风险吗？这是我从伊拉克人那里经常听到的。如果我们的孩子被蝎子咬伤快死了，我们该怎么办？那时我们可以违反宵禁吗？

00:28:58.580 --> 00:29:13.440
<v Stephanie Foo>The curfew was imposed by the Americans. And he couldn't promise that an Iraqi family traveling to the hospital to save their kid would not get shot by Americans who were trying to protect themselves from suicide bombers. So when Iraqis asked him this question?
<v Stephanie Foo>宵禁是美国人实施的。他无法保证一个去医院救孩子的伊拉克家庭不会被那些试图保护自己免受自杀式炸弹袭击的美国人枪杀。所以当伊拉克人问他这个问题时？

00:29:13.440 --> 00:29:45.280
<v Michael Pitre>The answer you want to give them is, yes, please, bring us your kid. Let us help. The actual answer is you're taking your life in your hands. And so that's the story you don't want to tell. Yeah, scorpions are not just this innocuous-- a scorpion bite for an Iraqi family causes an impossible decision and puts Marines in impossible situations. And for years, we were faced with that.
<v Michael Pitre>你想给出的答案是：是的，请带上你的孩子。让我们帮忙。但实际的答案是：你在拿自己的生命冒险。这就是那个你不想讲的故事。是的，蝎子对于伊拉克家庭来说不仅仅是无害的——蝎子咬伤会导致绝望的抉择，并让陆战队员陷入绝望的境地。多年来，我们一直面临这种情况。

00:29:45.280 --> 00:30:01.540
<v Stephanie  Foo>But Fred the Scorpion, the decoy story, worked. Everyone at the table laughed. Erin relaxed. Two months later, she quit her job and moved across the country to be with him.
<v Stephanie  Foo>但蝎子弗雷德，那个诱饵故事，奏效了。桌上的每个人都笑了。艾琳放松了。两个月后，她辞职并搬到全国各地和他在一起。

00:30:01.540 --> 00:30:25.470
<v Stephanie  Foo>Nearly 10% of Americans served in the Second World War. Back then, you very likely had a co-worker, a son, a neighbor who was serving in the war effort. In Vietnam, far fewer served. 2% of the population. Today, only about half a percent of our population has served in Iraq and Afghanistan, experiencing something that's mostly invisible to the rest of us.
<v Stephanie  Foo>近10%的美国人参加了第二次世界大战。那时，你很可能有个同事、儿子或邻居正在为战争出力。在越南战争中，参战人数要少得多。占人口的2%。今天，只有大约0.5%的美国人参加过伊拉克和阿富汗战争，经历了一些对我们大多数人来说几乎不可见的事情。

00:30:25.470 --> 00:31:01.900
<v Stephanie  Foo>So when people ask these seemingly simple questions about the war at a Christmas party, vets today have to figure out how to communicate this very large, nuanced, morally complicated thing to people who are probably coming to the conversation knowing nothing at all. Silence is an option a lot of vets go with. There's also dodging the question entirely, or the whole gory shock and awe method. The funny stories work around was Mike's solution. And for years, it worked. Sort of. There was a downside.
<v Stephanie  Foo>所以当人们在圣诞派对上问这些关于战争的看似简单的问题时，今天的退伍军人必须找出如何将这种非常宏大、细腻、道德复杂的议题传达给那些对此一无所知的人。保持沉默是许多退伍军人的选择。还有完全回避问题，或者采取那种血腥的“震惊与敬畏”方法。讲有趣的故事是迈克的解决方案。而且多年来，它奏效了。某种程度上。有一个负面影响。

00:31:01.900 --> 00:31:19.580
<v Stephanie  Foo>Mike came home from his final deployment in 2007. Then he and Erin moved to Louisiana to start their lives together. Both of them went back to school, they held dinner parties, went to shows. Everything seemed fantastic. But Erin was disturbed by the fact that Mike could be distant.
<v Stephanie  Foo>迈克于2007年结束最后一次部署回家。然后他和艾琳搬到了路易斯安那州开始他们的生活。他们都回到学校，举办晚宴，去看演出。一切看起来都很棒。但艾琳对迈克有时会变得疏离感到不安。

00:31:19.580 --> 00:31:41.220
<v Stephanie  Foo>Every once in a while, when he got really drunk, he'd lose his temper and start yelling. He often couldn't sleep and had trouble making friends. At those dinner parties, he could still make people laugh by telling those funny stories about the war. But Erin says they were always the same few stories, and he'd just held them over and over and over again. Here's Erin.
<v Stephanie  Foo>偶尔，当他喝得很醉时，他会失去控制并开始大喊大叫。他经常睡不着觉，也很难交到朋友。在那些晚宴上，他仍然可以通过讲那些战争中有趣的故事让人们发笑。但艾琳说那总是同样的那几个故事，他翻来覆去讲了又讲。这是艾琳。

00:31:41.220 --> 00:31:53.330
<v Erin>I could recognize that it was a mask, that it wasn't the real thing. I mean, he was vaudeville. You know, and I was guilty of being like, Mike, tell us. Tell us the time with the POO.
<v Erin>我能看出那是一个面具，那不是真实的他。我的意思是，他就像个杂耍艺人。你知道，我也曾犯过错，说：“迈克，给我们讲讲吧。给我们讲讲那个关于POO的故事。”

00:31:53.330 --> 00:31:53.750
<v Erin>[LAUGHING]
<v Erin>[笑声]

00:31:53.750 --> 00:32:08.580
<v Erin>But like, I would hear people ask him questions. And I would catch him telling decoy stories. I think he just wanted a break, and he wanted to live, and he wanted to be happy, which was OK. Until it wasn't.
<v Erin>但我会听到人们问他问题。我会发现他在讲诱饵故事。我想他只是想休息一下，他想生活，他想快乐，这没问题。直到有一天出了问题。

00:32:08.580 --> 00:32:21.800
<v Stephanie  Foo>A couple of years later, on a chilly evening, one of Mike's best friends from college visited the two of them in Louisiana. The friend was spending the night, and they were all on his porch at 2:00 AM drinking. His friend was talking.
<v Stephanie  Foo>几年后，在一个寒冷的夜晚，迈克的一位最好的大学朋友到路易斯安那州拜访他们。朋友在那里过夜，他们都在凌晨2点在门廊上喝酒。朋友在说话。

00:32:21.800 --> 00:32:41.930
<v Michael Pitre>And I think he kind of said almost apropos of nowhere, you know 5,000 people dying in a war is not really that much, historically speaking. And I lost control of myself. Before I knew it, I was in his face screaming with a finger pointed right at his face, and I had to be physically pulled away by two other people who were there.
<v Michael Pitre>我想他不知怎么地说了一句，差不多是突然冒出来的：你知道，历史上5000人在战争中死亡并不是什么大事。我失去了控制。在我意识到之前，我就已经冲到他面前，手指指着他的脸大叫，最后被在场的另外两个人强行拉开。

00:32:41.930 --> 00:32:51.730
<v Michael Pitre>I think what I was screaming at him was, yeah, 5,000 Americans, but you don't care at all about the 100,000 Iraqis, do you? Is that what you're telling me?
<v Michael Pitre>我想我当时对他大喊的是：是的，5000名美国人，但你一点也不在乎10万伊拉克人，对吧？这就是你要告诉我的吗？

00:32:51.730 --> 00:32:57.650
<v Stephanie Foo>Actually, the number is really in dispute. You see estimates between 115,000 and 500,000 deaths.
<v Stephanie Foo>事实上，死亡数字确实存在争议。你看到的估计数字在11.5万到50万之间。

00:32:57.650 --> 00:33:09.860
<v Stephanie Foo>Do you think that your friend might have been more careful with his choice of words if you had not projected a level of chill about your experiences, you know?
<v Stephanie Foo>你觉得如果你没有表现出对你的经历如此冷漠，你的朋友会不会在措辞上更谨慎一些？

00:33:09.860 --> 00:33:26.770
<v Michael Pitre>That's absolutely correct. Yeah. Yeah, my friend would have been more careful with his words had I not had such a perfectly cultivated veneer of chill that I'd spent years creating.
<v Michael Pitre>那绝对正确。是的。是的，如果我没有那样多年精心营造出来的冷漠外壳，我的朋友会在用词上更谨慎的。

00:33:26.770 --> 00:33:52.080
<v Stephanie  Foo>This kind of thing had happened a couple of times before, but this incident was the worst, and Erin was worried. She was the only person who knew how much he was suppressing. Because during Mike's second deployment, he'd handwritten her a letter every day telling her the truth, and his feelings about it, his fear, his grief.
<v Stephanie  Foo>这种事以前发生过几次，但这次事件是最糟糕的，艾琳很担心。她是唯一知道他压抑了多少的人。因为在迈克第二次部署期间，他每天都给她写信，告诉她真相，以及他的感受，他的恐惧，他的悲伤。

00:33:52.080 --> 00:34:15.050
<v Stephanie  Foo>But once he came home, Mike never mentioned any of these things again, not even to Erin. Whenever she tried to ask a question about them, he just said, my friends died there, so I don't want to talk about it. So the morning after Mike's outburst with his friend, Erin cornered Mike and told him, look, this is happening because you haven't told a real story about the war in years.
<v Stephanie  Foo>但一旦他回家，迈克就再也没提过这些事，即使是对艾琳。每当她试图问相关问题时，他总是说：“我的朋友死在那里，所以我不想谈论它。”所以迈克和朋友爆发冲突的第二天早上，艾琳把迈克逼到了角落告诉他：看，这之所以会发生，是因为你多年来都没讲过一个真实的关于战争的故事。

00:34:15.050 --> 00:34:24.470
<v Michael Pitre>The night before, which I was gone completely out of control, she was like, well, at least you were being real with him last night. At least you respect him that much.
<v Michael Pitre>昨晚，我彻底失控了，她说：好吧，至少你昨晚对他表现得很真实。至少你尊重他到了那个地步。

00:34:24.470 --> 00:34:28.290
<v Stephanie Foo>And how did you respond?
<v Stephanie Foo>你是怎么回应的？

00:34:28.290 --> 00:34:33.390
<v Michael Pitre>Uh, I don't think I did. She had me. She was right. There was no response to that.
<v Michael Pitre>呃，我不认为我有回应。她抓住了我。她是正确的。对此无言以对。

00:34:33.390 --> 00:34:41.070
<v Stephanie  Foo>Mike had thought the decoy stories had brought him closer to people. But he was now realizing, it might have been doing just the opposite.
<v Stephanie  Foo>迈克曾以为那些诱饵故事让他与人拉近了距离。但他现在意识到，情况可能恰恰相反。

00:34:41.070 --> 00:34:59.400
<v Michael Pitre>That's not a good feeling when you can't even communicate to someone the stories that are actually significant to you. You deceive them. You're lying to them. You're telling a story that's true, but that's not the story you want to tell.
<v Michael Pitre>当你甚至不能向人传达那些对你真正重要的故事时，这种感觉并不好。你在欺骗他们。你在对他们撒谎。你在讲一个真实的故事，但这并不是你想讲的故事。

00:34:59.400 --> 00:35:07.310
<v Michael Pitre>And over time, people think they know more and more about you, when they know less and less. And your friendships become kind of threadbare.
<v Michael Pitre>随着时间的推移，人们以为他们对你了解得越来越多，而其实他们了解的越来越少。而你们的友谊也变得有点破烂不堪。

00:35:07.310 --> 00:35:26.590
<v Stephanie Foo>Like his friendship with his best friend, Brock, who he'd known since he was 16. Brock was the one who asked him about the IEDs when he got back. And since then, he hadn't told him anything except decoy stories. But Brock did still ask questions. So the next time Brock asked a real question, Mike tried something different.
<v Stephanie Foo>就像他和他最好的朋友布洛克的友谊，他从16岁就认识他了。布洛克就是在他回来时问他IED问题的那个人。从那以后，他除了诱饵故事什么也没告诉过他。但布洛克确实还在问问题。所以当布洛克下次问一个真正的问题时，迈克尝试了一些不同的做法。

00:35:26.590 --> 00:35:40.460
<v Michael Pitre>He'd read an article about the recovery of bodies on the battlefield. And he wanted to know about how that worked. So I told him how that worked, how if there's a dead body, what the procedures are for removing them.
<v Michael Pitre>他读过一篇文章，关于战场上的尸体回收。他想知道那是怎么运作的。所以我告诉了他那是怎么运作的，如果有尸体，移除它们的程序是什么。

00:35:40.460 --> 00:36:06.360
<v Michael Pitre>The details of those types of procedures are horrible. So I told him one of the things you have to do sometimes is post a marine with a rifle to shoot dogs who are coming to try to carry away pieces of your dead friends. I told him that. And it was fine. And I realized it was fine, that I could talk to him.
<v Michael Pitre>那些程序细节很可怕。所以我告诉他，有时你必须做的一件事是派一名带步枪的陆战队员去射杀那些试图跑来叼走你死去的战友肢体的野狗。我告诉了他。结果没事。我意识到没关系，我能和他交谈。

00:36:06.360 --> 00:36:08.570
<v Stephanie Foo>What made it fine?
<v Stephanie Foo>是什么让这一切变得没关系？

00:36:08.570 --> 00:36:19.810
<v Michael   Pitre>What Brock did that was so important and so good, he didn't say anything about it. He just looked me in the eye until I was done.
<v Michael   Pitre>布洛克做的那件事非常重要且非常好，他对此什么也没说。他只是看着我的眼睛，直到我说完。

00:36:19.810 --> 00:36:25.150
<v Stephanie   Foo>He was signaling that he was willing to listen.
<v Stephanie   Foo>他是在传达他愿意倾听的信号。

00:36:25.150 --> 00:36:33.200
<v Michael Pitre>Yeah, and that he was not-- because when you see a monster, you look away. But he just kept looking at me in the eye.
<v Michael Pitre>是的，他并不是——因为当你看到怪物时，你会移开目光。但他只是直视我的眼睛。

00:36:33.200 --> 00:36:46.420
<v Stephanie            Foo>Since then, Mike has shared much more with the people he cares about.
<v Stephanie            Foo>从那以后，迈克和他在意的人分享了更多。

00:36:46.420 --> 00:36:49.660
<v Stephanie  Foo>So, do you still tell decoys now?
<v Stephanie  Foo>所以，你现在还会讲诱饵故事吗？

00:36:49.660 --> 00:36:52.640
<v Michael Pitre>All the time. Yeah. Want to hear another one?
<v Michael Pitre>一直都在讲。是的。想听另一个吗？

00:36:52.640 --> 00:36:54.730
<v Stephanie Foo>Sure. Of course I want a decoy story.
<v Stephanie Foo>当然。我当然想听诱饵故事。

00:36:54.730 --> 00:36:57.030
<v Michael Pitre>So I had this battalion executive officer, who--
<v Michael Pitre>所以我有这个营部的执行官，他——

00:36:57.030 --> 00:37:20.970
<v Stephanie Foo>The difference is now, he doesn't only tell decoy stories. He says when he decides which kind of story to tell, sometimes he thinks about the sign that used to hang above the door to the operations center in Iraq. It read, "What do I know, who needs to know it, and have I told them." And then, he begins his story.
<v Stephanie Foo>不同的是现在，他不只讲诱饵故事。他说，当他决定讲哪种故事时，有时他会想到过去挂在伊拉克作战中心门上的那个牌子。上面写着：“我知道什么，谁需要知道，我已经告诉他们了吗。”然后，他开始讲他的故事。

00:37:20.970 --> 00:38:11.560
<v Ira Glass>Stephanie Foo is one of the producers of our program. Regarding her story, Michael Pitre has written a novel. Stephanie says it's really good. It's called "Fives and 25's," which follows a group of Marines during and after the Iraq War. We'll have a link to it at our website.
<v Ira Glass>斯蒂芬妮·傅是我们节目的制作人之一。关于她的故事，迈克尔·皮特雷写了一本小说。斯蒂芬妮说那真的很好。书名是《5和25》，讲述了一群陆战队员在伊拉克战争期间及之后的故事。我们网站上会有链接。

00:38:11.560 --> 00:38:16.460
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC PLAYING]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐播放]

00:38:16.460 --> 00:38:29.400
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, a robot tries to get with the program. Get it? Get with the program? That's in a minute, from Chicago Public Radio, when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，一个机器人试图配合这个程序。懂吗？配合程序？稍后播出，来自芝加哥公共广播电台，节目继续。

00:38:29.400 --> 00:38:43.340
<v Ira Glass>"This American Life." I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our program, of course, we choose a theme, bring you different kinds of stories on that theme. Today's show, "Once More With Feeling," stories of people who decide to rethink the way they've been doing things. We'll try to get others to do that.
<v Ira Glass>《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。每周在我们的节目中，当然，我们都会选择一个主题，带来关于这个主题的不同类型的故事。今天的节目，《再次感受》，讲述人们决定重新思考他们做事方式的故事。我们也会尝试让其他人这样做。

00:38:43.340 --> 00:38:55.900
<v Ira Glass>We've arrived at Act Three of our program. Act Three, "You Had One Job." So we're going to end today's show with a machine, a machine that always does things the same way, because machines are designed and programmed to always do things the same way.
<v Ira Glass>我们的节目进入了第三幕。第三幕，“你只有一个任务”。所以我们要以一个机器结束今天的节目，一个总是以同样方式做事机器，因为机器被设计和编程为总是以同样方式做事。

00:38:55.900 --> 00:39:03.620
<v Ira Glass>This is a piece of fiction from somebody who's a journalist and a TV writer, named Scott Brown. This story was read for by actor Jeremy Shamos.
<v Ira Glass>这是一篇虚构作品，来自一位记者兼电视编剧，名叫斯科特·布朗。这个故事由演员杰里米·沙莫斯朗读。

00:39:03.620 --> 00:39:22.760
<v Jeremy  Shamos>I have one arm, because my job only takes one arm to do. And I only have one job. I like my job so much. The other officers at precinct nine don't know I like it. They don't know I like anything.
<v Jeremy  Shamos>我只有一只手臂，因为我的工作只需要一只手臂。而且我只有一个任务。我非常喜欢我的工作。九号分局的其他警官不知道我喜欢它。他们不知道我喜欢任何东西。

00:39:22.760 --> 00:39:48.890
<v Jeremy  Shamos>I like them, though, especially my operator, Officer Brian Parisse, who the other officers call Gamergate, Indoor Kid, and Tech Support, and whom I called Brian, but not out loud, because I can only say three things out loud-- "Stay Back 1,000 Feet," "Follow Officer's Instructions," "You are in the blast zone."
<v Jeremy  Shamos>不过我喜欢他们，尤其是我的操作员，布莱恩·帕里斯警官，其他警官叫他“玩家门”、“室内小孩”和“技术支持”，我叫他布莱恩，但不是大声叫，因为我大声只能说三句话——“保持1000英尺距离”、“遵循警官指示”、“你处在爆炸区”。

00:39:48.890 --> 00:40:07.530
<v Jeremy  Shamos>When I'm not doing my job, which is most of the time, I'm in the break room. I'm supposed to be in the vehicle bay, but someone thought it would be funny to put me in the break room, next to the coffee machine wearing a Carolina Panthers hat. The hat is to differentiate me from the coffee machine.
<v Jeremy  Shamos>当我没工作时，大多数时间都在休息室里。我应该在车辆库，但有人觉得把我放在休息室，咖啡机旁边，戴着卡罗莱纳黑豹队帽子会很有趣。这帽子是为了把我与咖啡机区分开。

00:40:07.530 --> 00:40:40.710
<v Jeremy  Shamos>That's a joke. I am nothing like the coffee machine. It weighs nine pounds. I weigh 900. It has rubber nubs for feet and doesn't move. I have six prehensile AT treads, and can travel up to 18 miles per hour, and I have a hat. My arm's been outstretched so I can give high fives and handshakes. Officers love those. In exchange, they give me a "What up, MILES?" or a "Peace out, MILES."
<v Jeremy  Shamos>这是个笑话。我一点也不像咖啡机。它重9磅。我重900磅。它有橡胶脚垫不会动。我有六条预抓式AT履带，时速可达18英里，而且我还有一顶帽子。我的手臂一直伸着，所以我可以击掌和握手。警官们喜欢这些。作为交换，他们会对我喊“怎么了，MILES？”或者“再见，MILES”。

00:40:40.710 --> 00:41:07.290
<v Jeremy  Shamos>I'm MILES, and I'm very good at handshakes. Just OK at high-fives. Getting better at fist bumps. I can live with the hat. I know the hat looks stupid because my eye is better than the officers know, and because I have limited learning, a software upgrade only Brian knows about, and because there's a mirror in the break room.
<v Jeremy  Shamos>我是MILES，我非常擅长握手。击掌只是一般般。碰拳正变得越来越好。我可以忍受那顶帽子。我知道那帽子看起来很蠢，因为我的眼睛比警官们知道的要敏锐得多，而且因为我有有限的自学能力，一个只有布莱恩知道的软件升级，而且休息室里有镜子。

00:41:07.290 --> 00:41:51.410
<v Jeremy  Shamos>But mostly, I just like my job. It's simple. There's unsecured ordinance, what Brian calls a bomb. I come between the unsecured ordinance and the people. Either one, I neutralize the ordinance, or two, I don't stop the ordinance and I'm superficially damaged, or three, I intentionally detonate the ordinance, and I'm superficially damaged. I'm designed to be superficially damaged. In all three scenarios, nobody gets hurt. That's the idea.
<v Jeremy  Shamos>但大多数时候，我只是喜欢我的工作。很简单。有未固定的军械，布莱恩称之为炸弹。我出现在未固定军械和人群之间。要么我让军械失效，要么我没能阻止军械导致我表面受损，或者我故意引爆军械，导致我表面受损。我的设计就是为了表面受损。在这三种情况下，没人会受伤。这就是目的。

00:41:51.410 --> 00:42:14.230
<v Jeremy  Shamos>Last summer, there was an oily package addressed to "Those In Charge" left outside a community health center. As I was unloaded from my trailer, I saw a girl around six entering the blast zone with a jump rope. She had tight black braids in straight rows that ended in bright blue beads with tiny cartoon fish on them. No one else noticed her.
<v Jeremy  Shamos>去年夏天，一个油腻的包裹寄给“负责人”，被留在社区健康中心外。当我从拖车上卸下时，我看到一个六岁左右的女孩带着跳绳进入爆炸区。她梳着紧紧的黑色辫子，发梢上有明亮的蓝色珠子，上面画着小卡通鱼。没有其他人注意到她。

